===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|zoology}} An [[organ]] or [[other]] [[body part]] that is or [[can]] be [[turned]] [[inside out]], [[especially]] an [[anterior]] [[portion]] of [[some]] [[annelid]] [[worm]]s [[capable]] of [[retraction]].
#* '''1883''', E. Ray Lankester, ''Encyclopaedia Britannica'', Vol. XVI, p. 652, s.v. "Mollusca":
#*: Important distinctions which obtain amongst the various ‘'''introverts'''’ or [[intro-]] and [[e-]]versible tubes so frequently met with in animal bodies.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1990|title=Seaside Naturalist  |author=Deborah A. Coulombe  |page=76 |ISBN= 0671765035|passage=Peanut worms (phylum Sipunculida) are drab-colored bottom dwellers, most of which are less then four inches long. Their body is divided into two sections: the trunk and the '''introvert'''. The '''introvert''', the narrow neck-like anterior section, is used as a probe. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006 |title=Nematodes, Leeches, and Other Worms  |author= |page=38 |ISBN=0756516153 |passage=When a peanut worm feeds, it extends and waves its '''introvert''' so that the mouth at the tip wanders across the seabed. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009 |title=Kingdoms and Domains: An Illustrated Guide to the Phyla of Life on Earth |author=Lynn Marguli; Michael J Chapman  |page= |ISBN=0080920144 |passage=The '''introvert''' is everted by hydraulic pressure exerted by contraction of body muscles on the coelomic fluid. }}
# {{lb|en|psychology}} An [[introverted]] [[person]]: [[one]] who is [[considered]] [[more]] [[thoughtful]] than [[social]], with a [[personality]] [[more]] [[inwardly]] than [[outwardly]] [[directed]]; one who often prefers to have time in non-social situations.
#* '''1916''', Constance Ellen Long trans. {{w|Carl Jung}} as ''Collected Papers on Analytical Psychology'', p. 349:
#*: An [[extravert|Extravert]] can hardly conceive the necessity which compels the '''Introvert''' to conquer the world by means of a system.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|month=April|author=Phyllis Blanchard|chapter=A Psycho-Analytic Study of August Comte|title=American Journal of Psychology|page=163
|passage=In order to understand the marked contract between Comte's mental attitude during his early years and that of his later life, we must keep in mind [[:w:Carl Jung|Jung]]'s hypothesis of the two psychological types, the '''introvert''' and extrovert,—the thinking type and the feeling type.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=May 21 1920|journal=Challenge|page=44
|passage=All works of the imagination are conceived by men of the '''introvert''' type.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1925|author=Charles Fox|title=Educational Psychology|page=254
|passage=The '''introvert''' abstracts from the object and deals with it by concepts concentrating upon the inner world of thought.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 January 13|author=Paul Clifton|title=Delivering a basis for rail changes|journal=RAIL, issue 922|page=30|text=This softly spoken but fluent communicator describes himself as an '''introvert''' who prefers not to shout from the rooftops.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively|proscribed}} A [[reserved]] [[person]].

====Usage notes====
Psychologists tend to distinguish between [[introversion]], which defines one's inherent social preferences and exists on a spectrum of behavior with [[ambiversion]] and [[extraversion]], from [[shyness]] and other manifestations of [[social anxiety]] or [[trauma]]. Popular use tends to lump such behaviors together and sometimes pejoratively consider them [[withdrawn]] or [[antisocial]].

